A||||3||mshill||Dialectric grease is not heatsink compound!!!||||4||
Z||000000||mshill||12-23-1999||01:05 AM||||I picked up an ignition module at the boneyard on Monday.  Stopped by Autozone on the way home to get some heatsink compound for the back of the module.  The guy at Autozone sold me dialectric grease instead.  I installed the module with plenty of grease (bad thing) and since it worked I left it in and kept the 3 month old one in the trunk with some tools (good idea).<P>Well, today I was cruising along at 70 mph in the fast lane on I80 and POOF! everything goes dead, tach does to zero and I know exactly what it is.  Well, I started thinking about what kills these things, HEAT! So, I finally figure out that dialectric grease is to conduct electricity,and propbably not heat.  On the contrary, it likely has some heat insulating properties.  <P>I switched out the modules and it started right up.  At the first off ramp was one of my favorite electronics stores where I picked up some silicone heatsink compound.  I cleaned all the dialectric grease off and put the heatsink compound on and off I went with no problems.  Just too bad I have to scrounge another backup module at the boneyard now.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-23-1999||02:10 AM||||I seen it in my haynes manual for my tbird turbo coupe 87, that you should apply  <BR>dielectric grease to the back of the ignition module.  I know that ford had problems with the modules and they would go out.  There is some law suit that california is sueing  ford because they were aware of the problem for many years.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GREENDANO||12-25-1999||04:45 PM||||If the module was an aftermarket module it failed because they're junk.  There's no substitute for OEM when it comes to replacement ignition modules.  Fiero engine compartments are hotter than regular front engined cars that have airflow all the time, which causes premature aftermarket ignition module failure.<BR>  Dielectric silicone is a heat sink compound and is electrically non-conductive for the most part.  It is exactly what you get in a little packet with a new module from GM.  The biggest difference with the grease you got and the clear stuff GM uses is color and viscosity.  More or less.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||13
Z||000003||theogre||12-26-1999||01:23 PM||||Dialectric grease is generally a silcone based compound. It is inteneded to seal out water and electrical insulate conectors. It can also be used as heatsink grease but isn't really ment for it.<P>Heat sink grease is also a Silicone based compound, but it also has Zink Oxide added to improve it's thermal transfer. (There are other type but the Si/Zink is most common.)<P>If you would like to learn more about using heatsinks, in this case as they relate to Intel CPU systems but the information tranfers to all heatsink setups, go here.<P>home.sprynet.com/~theogre<P>The info is in the tech center.||||reg||1
